      {
        "id": "step_03_valves",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_hololens",
        "location": "main_workstation",
        "predicate": "valve_labels_image",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Four Heart Valves and Their Positions",
          "description": "Four one-way valves maintain blood flow in the correct direction through the heart and prevent backflow during the cardiac cycle. The TRICUSPID VALVE separates the right atrium from the right ventricle and opens to allow blood to flow downward when the atrium contracts. The PULMONARY VALVE (also called the pulmonary semilunar valve) sits at the exit of the right ventricle and opens when the ventricle contracts to let blood into the pulmonary arteries headed to the lungs; it closes to prevent backflow. The MITRAL VALVE (also called the bicuspid valve) separates the left atrium from the left ventricle and opens when the atrium contracts to allow oxygenated blood to flow downward. The AORTIC VALVE (also called the aortic semilunar valve) sits at the exit of the left ventricle and opens when the ventricle contracts to let blood into the aorta headed to the body; it also closes to prevent backflow. The tricuspid and mitral valves are atrioventricular (AV) valves; the pulmonary and aortic valves are semilunar valves."
        },
