        "id": "step_04_vessels",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_hololens",
        "location": "main_workstation",
        "predicate": "vessel_identification_image",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Major Blood Vessels Entering and Leaving the Heart",
          "description": "Six major blood vessels connect the heart to the lungs and systemic circulation. VENOUS RETURN: The SUPERIOR VENA CAVA carries deoxygenated blood from the upper body (head, arms, chest) into the right atrium. The INFERIOR VENA CAVA carries deoxygenated blood from the lower body (abdomen, legs) into the right atrium. PULMONARY CIRCUIT: The RIGHT and LEFT PULMONARY ARTERIES carry deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the left and right lungs, respectively. The PULMONARY VEINS (four total: right superior, right inferior, left superior, left inferior) carry freshly oxygenated blood from the lungs back to the left atrium. SYSTEMIC CIRCUIT: The AORTA is the massive elastic artery that carries oxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the entire body. It branches into many smaller arteries that distribute blood throughout all tissues. Pay special attention to the size difference: the aorta is much larger than the pulmonary arteries because it must deliver blood at higher pressure throughout the entire body."
        },
