          "title": "Understanding the Pulmonary and Systemic Circuits",
          "description": "The heart drives two distinct but interconnected circulatory loops. THE PULMONARY CIRCUIT is the shorter, lower-pressure loop. It begins when the right ventricle pumps deoxygenated blood into the pulmonary arteries, which carry it to the lungs. In the lungs, carbon dioxide diffuses out and oxygen diffuses in. Oxygenated blood then returns to the left atrium via the pulmonary veins. The pulmonary circuit is a low-pressure system because the lungs are very close to the heart and lung tissue is delicate. THE SYSTEMIC CIRCUIT is the longer, higher-pressure loop. It begins when the left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta, which branches extensively to reach every tissue in the body. Cells extract oxygen and nutrients while releasing carbon dioxide and metabolic wastes. Deoxygenated blood returns to the right atrium via the vena cava, completing the circuit. The systemic circuit operates at much higher pressure because blood must travel long distances and perfuse many tissues against gravity and resistance. This is why the left ventricle is so much more muscular than the right ventricle: it must generate 5 to 6 times higher pressure to drive systemic circulation compared to the pulmonary circulation. Both loops function simultaneously, with the right and left sides working in parallel to maintain continuous circulation."
