          "title": "Why the Left Ventricle Has a Thicker Myocardium",
          "description": "One of the most striking features of cardiac anatomy is the dramatic difference in wall thickness between the left and right ventricles. The LEFT VENTRICLE has a myocardial wall that is approximately 2.5 to 3 times thicker than the right ventricle. This difference reflects a fundamental principle in muscle physiology: thicker muscle tissue can generate greater force and pressure. The left ventricle must generate very high pressure\u2014about 120 mmHg (systolic) to pump blood throughout the entire body against gravity and tissue resistance. This blood must travel through thousands of kilometers of arteries, arterioles, and capillaries to reach every cell. In contrast, the RIGHT VENTRICLE only needs to generate about 25 mmHg to send blood to the nearby lungs, which are a low-resistance system. The increased myocardial mass of the left ventricle provides the contractile force needed for powerful, sustained contractions. This is a beautiful example of form following function: the structural thickness of the ventricular wall is directly related to the physiological demands placed on each ventricle. When the left ventricle is damaged or weakened (as in left ventricular hypertrophy or heart failure), its ability to generate sufficient pressure is compromised, leading to serious consequences for systemic perfusion."
