          "title": "Valve Function and Prevention of Backflow",
          "description": "Heart valves are elegant one-way gates that maintain unidirectional blood flow and prevent dangerous backflow during the cardiac cycle. ATRIOVENTRICULAR VALVES (Tricuspid and Mitral): These valves sit between the atria and ventricles. When an atrium contracts, the valve opens, allowing blood to flow downward into the ventricle. When the ventricle contracts, the valve snaps shut, preventing backflow of blood back into the atrium. The valve leaflets are held in place by strong collagen cords called chordae tendineae, which attach to papillary muscles in the ventricle wall. These structures prevent the valve from flipping backward during ventricular contraction. SEMILUNAR VALVES (Pulmonary and Aortic): These valves sit at the exit of each ventricle. When a ventricle contracts, pressure inside increases, forcing the semilunar valve open and propelling blood into the arteries. When the ventricle relaxes, blood pressure in the artery exceeds the pressure in the ventricle, and the valve snaps shut, sealing the opening and preventing backflow of blood back into the ventricle. This closure is audible as part of the second heart sound (S2, or 'dup'). When valves become damaged or scarred\u2014whether from infection (endocarditis), rheumatic fever, degenerative disease, or other causes\u2014they may leak (regurgitation) or narrow (stenosis), compromising the heart's pumping efficiency. Understanding normal valve structure and function is essential for recognizing and diagnosing cardiac pathology."
        },
